/// Sprint 1.7 — 360 Evaluation. This file now declares ENUMS ONLY. The three models it used to hold
/// were removed by ownership flip #67; see the block below before adding anything here.
enum ReviewCycleStatus {
  draft
  open
  closed
  published
}

enum RaterRelationship {
  self
  manager
  peer
  direct_report
}

enum RaterAssignmentStatus {
  pending
  submitted
}


// ── OWNERSHIP FLIP #67 (2026-08-06) — DO NOT RE-ADD THESE MODELS ───────────────────────────────
//
// `ReviewCycle` / `RaterAssignment` / `RaterResponse` used to live here. The tables `review_cycles`,
// `rater_assignments` and `rater_responses` STILL EXIST IN PRODUCTION and still hold data — they are
// now owned by EF Core (`Evaluation360WriteDbContext` / `Evaluation360ReadDbContext` in
// services/Tims.Platform/src/Tims.Infrastructure/Evaluation360/), which is the sole writer, sole
// reader and sole schema authority. See `efcore[]` in docs/architecture/table-ownership.md and the
// §7e transcript in docs/architecture/csharp-migration/ownership-flip-runbook.md.
//
// Re-adding a model here is a `cross-owner collision` in `scripts/table-ownership.mjs` (red build),
// and it re-arms the §2 DROP TABLE hazard from the other direction.
//
// The repo's bootstrap definition of the three tables is
// services/Tims.Platform/db/flip-ddl/evaluation360.sql — apply it after `prisma db push` when
// bootstrapping a dev database. (Unlike flips #2 and #3, an original `CREATE TABLE` also survives at
// packages/db/prisma/migrations/20260713150000_add_evaluation360/migration.sql, so runbook §0 P8 was
// NOT live here. The flip-ddl file is preferred anyway: it is extracted from the committed prod
// baseline rather than from migration source, and it is idempotent, atomic and GRANT-complete.)
//
// THE ENUMS ABOVE ARE DELIBERATELY KEPT, and that is a decision, not leftovers:
//   - `packages/db/src/index.ts:16-18` re-exports all three from `@tims/db`; deleting them is a
//     public-API change to that package for no functional gain.
//   - The Postgres types are a cross-stack contract. `scripts/parity/seed.ts:799,817,1388` casts to
//     `"ReviewCycleStatus"` / `"RaterRelationship"` / `"RaterAssignmentStatus"` in raw SQL, and the
//     EF entities read the same columns. `tests/db/evaluation360-schema.test.ts` pins their exact
//     value lists, which stays a live guarantee about production after the flip.
//   - Keeping them narrows the §2 hazard: `prisma migrate diff --from-schema-datasource` will emit a
//     `DROP TABLE` for the three tables, but no `DROP TYPE` for these.
// The cost, stated so it is not discovered later: `prisma db push` still CREATEs these three types on
// a fresh database, i.e. Prisma still emits a little DDL for an EF-owned domain. Both orders are safe
// — evaluation360.sql guards every `CREATE TYPE` on `pg_type` — and both were exercised on a scratch
// cluster (runbook §7e). Changing an enum VALUE here would be a real schema change to an EF-owned
// domain and must go through docs/architecture/ddl-governance.md instead.
